====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|weather}} Any [[disturbed#Adjective|disturbed]] [[state#Noun|state]] of the [[atmosphere]] [[cause#Verb|causing]] [[destructive]] or [[unpleasant]] [[weather#Noun|weather]], especially one [[affect#Verb|affecting]] the [[earth#Noun|earth]]'s [[surface#Noun|surface]] [[involve|involving]] [[strong#Adjective|strong]] [[wind#Noun|winds]] ([[lead#Verb|leading]] to [[high#Adjective|high]] [[wave#Noun|waves]] at [[sea]]) and usually [[lightning#Noun|lightning]], [[thunder#Noun|thunder]], and [[precipitation]]; a [[tempest#Noun|tempest]].
#: {{ux|en|The boat was torn to pieces in the '''storm''', and nobody survived.}}
#* {{RQ:Garnier Kyd Cornelia|sig=A2|verso=1|page=16|passage=Thou toyl'ſt in perrill, and the vvindie '''ſtorme''', / Doth topſide-turuey toſſe thee as thou floteſt.}}
#* {{RQ:Garnier Kyd Cornelia|sig=K3|verso=1|page=84|passage=But ſeeing that there the murdring Enemie, / Peſle-meſle, purſued them like a '''ſtorme''' of hayle, / They gan retyre vvhere ''Iuba'' vvas encampt; {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 2 Q1|act=II|scene=i|page=34|passage=[W]e heare this fearefull tempeſt ſing, / Yet ſeeke no ſhelter to auoid the '''ſtorme''': / We ſee the vvind ſit ſore vpon our ſailes.}}
#* {{RQ:Donne Poems|poem=The Storme|page=57|passage=And vvhat at firſt vvas call'd a guſt, the ſame / Hath novv a '''ſtormes''', anon a tempeſts name.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Much Ado About Nothing|act=V|scene=iv|page=121|column=1|passage=[W]hy vvhat's the matter? / That you haue ſuch a Februarie face, / So full of froſt, of '''ſtorme''', and clovvdineſſe.|footer=A figurative use.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|act=II|scene=ii|page=9|column=1|passage=Here's neither buſh, nor ſhrub to beare off any vveather at all: and another '''Storme''' brevving, I heare it ſing ith' vvinde: yond ſame blacke cloud, yond huge one, lookes like a foule bumbard that vvould ſhed his licquor: {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Herbert Travaile|edition=4th|page=11|passage=[D]uring this late tuffon, lightning vvas ſeen to fall and hang like fire, ſometimes to skip to and fro about the Yards and Tackling of our Ships. {{...}} [S]ome call ''{{w|Hermes}}'' fire; [[w:Erasmus of Formia|Saint ''Elmo'']] others; {{...}} vvithall believing, that vvhen tvvo are ſeen, they foretel ''Halcyon'' vveather and ſafety; if one, it imports danger; but three threaten '''ſtorms''' and ſhipvvrack. ''Sed non ego credulus illis'' [But I am not credulous of them], vvell-knovving that theſe Meteors are no other than natural Exhalations.}}
#* {{RQ:Tennyson Princess|page=111|passage=When '''storm''' is on the heights, and right and left / Suck'd from the dark heart of the long hills roll / The torrents, dash'd to the vale: and yet her will / Bred will in me to overcome it or fall.}}
#* {{RQ:Tennyson In Memoriam|canto=XXXIII|page=52|passage=O thou that after toil and '''storm''' / Mayst seem to have reach'd a purer air, / Whose faith has centre everywhere, / Nor cares to fix itself to form, {{...}}|footer=A figurative use.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=w:Donald Worster|title=A Drier and Hotter Future|magazine=w:American Scientist|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20120110021957/https://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/a-drier-and-hotter-future|location=New Haven, Conn.|publisher=[[w:Sigma Xi|Sigma Xi, the Scientific Research Society]]|month=January|year=2012|volume=100|issue=1|page=70|issn=0003-0996|oclc=891112584|passage=Phoenix and Lubbock are both caught in severe drought, and it is going to get much worse. We may see many such [dust] '''storms''' in the decades ahead, along with species extinctions, radical disturbance of ecosystems, and intensified social conflict over land and water. Welcome to the Anthropocene, the epoch when humans have become a major geological and climatic force.}}
## {{lb|en|by extension}} A [[heavy#Adjective|heavy]] [[fall#Noun|fall]] of precipitation ([[hail#Noun|hail]], [[rain#Noun|rain]], or [[snow#Noun|snow]]) or [[bout]] of lightning and thunder without strong winds; a [[hail storm]], [[rainstorm]], [[snowstorm]], or [[thunderstorm]].
## {{lb|en|by extension}} {{synonym of|en|cyclone|t=a weather [[phenomenon]] [[consist]]ing of a [[system]] of winds [[rotate#Verb|rotating]] around a [[center#Noun|centre]] of [[low#Adjective|low]] [[atmospheric pressure]]}}
## {{lb|en|by extension|Canada|Scotland|US|dated}} A [[period#Noun|period]] of [[frosty]] and/or [[snowy#Adjective|snowy]] weather.
## {{lb|en|meteorology}} A disturbed state of the atmosphere between a [[severe]] or strong [[gale#Noun|gale]] and a [[hurricane]] on the [[modern#Adjective|modern]] [[Beaufort scale]], with a wind [[speed#Noun|speed]] of between 89 and 102 [[kilometre]]s per [[hour]] (55–63 [[mile]]s per hour; 10 on the [[scale#Noun|scale]], known as a "storm" or whole gale), or of between 103 and 117 kilometres per hour (64–72 miles per hour; 11 on the scale, known as a "[[violent#Adjective|violent]] storm").
# {{lb|en|figurative}}
## A heavy [[expulsion]] or fall of [[thing]]s (as [[blow#Noun|blows]], [[object#Noun|objects]] which are [[throw#Verb|thrown]], etc.).
##: {{ux|en|a '''storm''' of bullets}}
##* {{RQ:Tasso Fairfax Godfrey of Bulloigne|book=11|stanza=34|page=202|passage=''Adrastus'' firſt aduanſt his creſt aloft, / And boldly gan a ſtrong ſcalado reare, / And through the falling '''ſtorme''' did vpward clime / Of ſtones, dartes, arrovves, fire, pitch and lime: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=VI|lines=544–546|page=172|passage=[F]or this day will pour down, / If I conjecture aught, no drizling ſhowr, / But ratling '''ſtorm''' of Arrows barbd with fire.|year=1873}}
##* {{RQ:Scott Harold|stanza=XV|page=194|passage=Then rose / His mace, and with a '''storm''' of blows / The mortal and the demon close.}}
##* {{RQ:Tennyson Poems 1842|volume=II|poem=St. Simeon Stylites|page=53|passage=I will not cease to grasp the hope I hold / Of saintdom, and to clamour, mourn and sob, / Battering the gates of heaven with '''storms''' of prayer, / Have mercy, Lord, and take away my sin.|footer=A figurative use.}}
##* {{quote-web|en|author=HarryBlank|work=w:SCP Foundation|title=Off Track|date=22 September 2023|url=https://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/25-off-track|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20240525063003/https://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/25-off-track|archivedate=25 May 2024|accessdate=25 May 2024|text="Sorry about the bullet '''storm'''," Corbin said as they approached her living quarters. "I'm a bit of a bet-hedger these days."}}
## A violent [[agitation]] of [[human#Adjective|human]] [[society]]; a [[domestic#Adjective|domestic]], [[civil]], or [[political#Adjective|political]] [[commotion]].
##: {{synonyms|en|tempest|tumult}}
##: {{ux|en|The proposed reforms have led to a political '''storm'''.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Taming of the Shrew|act=I|scene=i|page=211|column=2|passage=Savv you no more? Mark'd you not hovv hir ſiſter / Began to ſcold, and raiſe vp ſuch a '''ſtorme''', / That moral eares might hardly indure the din.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-2|act=III|scene=i|page=133|column=1|passage=VVhiles I in Ireland nouriſh a mightie Band, / I vvill ſtirre vp in England ſome black '''Storme''', / Shall blovve then thouſand Soules to Heauen, or Hell: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Bacon Works|volume=IV|subtitle=The Charge of Sir Francis Bacon, Knight, His Majesty's Attorney-General, Touching Duels;{{nb...|upon an Information in the Star-Chamber against Priest and Wright.}}|year=1613|page=400|passage=[When] private men begin once to presume to give law to themselves, and to right their own wrongs, no man can foresee the dangers and inconveniences that may arise and multiply thereupon. It may cause sudden '''storms''' in Court, to the disturbance of his Majesty, and unsafety of his person.|footer=The spelling has been modernized.}}
##* {{RQ:Wesley Hymns|poem=Acts iv. 29.|stanza=VI|page=203|passage=Bold may I vvax, exceeding bold / My high Commiſſion to perform, / Nor ſhrink thy harſheſt Truths t' unfold, / But more than meet the gathering '''Storm'''.}}
##* {{RQ:Macaulay History of England|volume=II|chapter=X|page=623|passage=But they were all speedily thrown into the shade by two younger Whigs &#91;{{w|Charles Montagu, 1st Earl of Halifax}} and {{w|John Somers, 1st Baron Somers}}&#93;, who, on this great day, took their seats for the first time, who soon rose to the highest honors of the state, who weathered together the fiercest '''storms''' of faction, {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Dickens Bleak House|chapter=Deportment|page=131|passage=Pa told me, only yesterday morning, (and dreadfully unhappy he is) that he couldn't weather the '''storm'''. {{...}} I should like to make out how Pa ''is'' to weather the '''storm'''. I declare if I was Pa, I'd run away!}}
## A violent commotion or [[outbreak#Noun|outbreak]] of [[sound#Noun|sounds]], [[speech#Noun|speech]], [[thought#Noun|thoughts]], etc.; also, an [[outpouring]] of [[emotion]].
##: {{coi|en|a '''storm''' of protest}}
##* {{RQ:King James Version|section=The Translators to the Reader|page=7|passage=[W]as there euer any thing proiected, that fauoured any vvay of nevvneſſe or renevving, but the ſame endured many a '''ſtorme''' of gaine-ſaying, or oppoſition?}}
##* {{RQ:Homer Chapman Odysseys|year=1857|book=9|page=211|lines=43–44|passage=With '''storms''' of whistlings then his flock he drave / Up to the mountains; {{...}}|footer=The spelling has been modernized.}}
##* {{RQ:Dryden Juvenal Satires|chapter=Persius|satire=1|page=6|passage=The Proſe is Fuſtian, and the Numbers lame. All Noiſe, and empty Pomp, a '''ſtorm''' of vvords, / Lab'ring vvith ſound, that little Sence affords.}}
##* {{RQ:Spectator|author=Addison|issue=407|date=17 June 1712|page=13|passage=[H]ow much more they would have been alarmed had they heard him actually throwing out such a '''storm''' of eloquence?}}
##* {{RQ:Thomson Spring|year=1768|page=13|lines=296–299|passage=Theſe, and a thouſand mixt emotions more, / From ever-changing vievvs of good and ill, / Form'd infinitely various, vex the mind / VVith endleſs '''ſtorm''': {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Cowper Poems|poem=Table Talk|page=26|passage=The ſtrings are ſvvept vvith ſuch a povv'r, ſo loud, / The '''ſtorm''' of muſic ſhakes th' aſtoniſh'd crovvd.}}
##* {{RQ:Tennyson Princess|page=117|passage=[Y]et a moment, and once more / The trumpet, and again: at which the '''storm''' / Of galloping hoofs bare on the ridge of spears / And riders front to front, until they closed / In the middle with the crash of shivering points, / And thunder.}}
##* {{RQ:Macaulay History of England|volume=I|chapter=IV|page=484|passage=He betrayed, however, no sign of fear or of shame, and faced the '''storm''' of invective which burst upon him for bar, bench, and witness box, with the insolence of despair.}}
##* {{RQ:Tennyson Enoch Arden|poem=Aylmer's Field|page=68|passage=Sir Aylmer reddening from the '''storm''' within, / Then broke all bonds of courtesy, {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Caine Manxman|chapter=IV|page=17|passage=When his playfellows ran after the dogs in their fanatic thirst, he ran too, but with a '''storm''' of other feelings.}}
## {{lb|en|pathology}} ''Chiefly with a [[qualify]]ing word'': a violent [[attack#Noun|attack]] of [[disease#Noun|diease]], [[pain#Noun|pain]], [[physiological]] [[reaction]]s, [[symptom]]s, etc.; a [[paroxysm]].
##: {{coi|en|asthmatic '''storm'''&emsp;&emsp;&emsp; [[cytokine storm|cytokine '''storm''']]}}
##* {{RQ:Cervantes Shelton Don Quixote|volume=I|chapter=Wherein are Rehearsed the Innumerable Misfortunes which Don-Quixote and His Good Squire Sancho Suffered in the Inne, which He to His Harme Thought to be a Castle|part=3|page=136|passage=He ſvvet and ſvvet againe vvith ſuch exceſſiue ſvvoonings, as not only himſelfe, but likevviſe all the beholders, did verily deeme, that his life vvas ending. This '''ſtorme''' and miſhappe endured about ſome tvvo hovvres, after vvhich hee remayned not cured as his Maſter, but ſo vveary and indiſpoſed, as hee vvas not able to ſtand.}}
# {{lb|en|Canada|US|chiefly|in the plural}} {{ellipsis of|en|storm window|t=a [[second#Adjective|second]] [[window#Noun|window]] ([[originally]] [[detachable#Adjective|detachable]]) [[attach]]ed on the [[exterior#Adjective|exterior]] [[side#Noun|side]] of a window in [[climate]]s with [[harsh#Adjective|harsh]] [[winter#Noun|winters]], to [[add#Verb|add]] an [[insulating#Adjective|insulating]] [[layer#Noun|layer]] of [[still#Adjective|still]] [[air#Noun|air]] between the [[outside#Noun|outside]] and [[inside#Noun|inside]]}}

