===Etymology 2===
The {{glossary|verb}} is derived from {{inh|en|enm|stormen|t=of the wind: to blow violently; to cause to roll or toss}},<ref name="MED">{{R:MED Online|entry=stormen|pos=v|id=MED43117}}</ref> from {{m|enm|storm|pos=noun}} (see [[#Etymology 1|etymology 1]]) + {{m|enm|-en|pos={{glossary|suffix}} forming the {{glossary|infinitive}}s of {{glossary|verb}}s}}.<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=-en|pos=''suf.''(3)|noformat=1|id=MED13518}}</ref><ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=v|date=July 2023|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=v}}</ref>

Compare {{cog|enm|sturmen|t=to attack (someone) with great force}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=sturmen|pos=v|id=MED43493}}</ref> from {{cog|ang|styrman|t=to rage, storm; to make a great noise, cry aloud, shout, storm}}, from {{cog|gmw-pro|*sturmijan|t=to storm}}, from {{cog|gem-pro|*sturmijaną|t=to storm}}, from {{m|gem-pro|*sturmaz|t=a storm}} (see etymology 1) + {{m|gem-pro|*-janą|pos=suffix forming {{glossary|causative}}s from {{glossary|strong verb}}s, with a sense of ‘to cause to do [''the action of the verb'']’}}.<ref>Compare {{R:OED Online|entry=† sturme|url=https://www.oed.com/dictionary/sturme_v|pos=v|date=July 2023}}</ref> The Middle English word did not survive into modern English.

The {{glossary|noun}} is derived from verb {{senseno|en|assault}} (“to violently assault (a fortified position or stronghold, a building, etc.) with the aim of gaining control of it”).<ref name="OED"/>

