## {{senseid|en|assault}}{{lb|en|chiefly|military}} To violently [[assault#Verb|assault]] (a [[fortified#Adjective|fortified]] [[position#Noun|position]] or [[stronghold]], a [[building#Noun|building]], etc.) with the [[aim#Noun|aim]] of [[gain#Verb|gaining]] [[control#Noun|control]] of it.
##: {{ux|en|the '''storming''' of the [[Bastille]]}}
##: {{ux|en|Troops '''stormed''' the complex.}}
##* {{RQ:Prior Poetical Works|volume=II|title=Horace|stanza=II|page=112|passage=All night beneath hard heavy arms to vvatch; / All day to mount the trench, to ſtorm the breach; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Irving Sketch Book|version=UK|volume=II|title=Philip of Pokanoket|pages=259–260|pageref=260|passage=The assailants were repulsed in their first attack, and several of their bravest officers were shot down in the act of '''storming''' the fortress, sword in hand.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=K. S. Karol|chapter=The August of the Ultra-Left|translator=Mervyn Jones|title=The Second Chinese Revolution{{nb...|Translated from the French by Mervyn Jones}}|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Hill and Wang|year=1974|page=278|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/secondchineserev0000karo/page/278/mode/1up|oclc=122392620|passage=The crowd was patient and never dreamed of '''storming''' Chungnanhai (which could scarcely have resisted a mass assault) and the most battle-tested groups made no attempt to send their commandos to kidnap the “highest leader.” Calm—if one may use the word—prevailed, and the group leaders were content to lead their followers in chanting slogans against [[w:Liu Shaoqi|Liu [Shaoqi]]] and quotations from [[w:Mao Zedong|Mao [Zedong]]]. The Chairman, like Vice-Chairman [[w:Lin Biao|Lin Piao]], had been away on a tour of inspection in the provinces since early July; at the time of the siege of Chungnanhai, he was in Wuhan.}}
### {{lb|en|figurative|often|poetic}} To assault or gain control or [[power#Noun|power]] over (someone's [[heart#Noun|heart]], [[mind#Noun|mind]], etc.).
###* {{quote-book|en|author={{w|Thomas Morell}} (lyrics); {{w|George Frideric Handel}} (music)|title=[[w:Theodora (Handel)|Theodora: An Oratorio{{nb...|Set to Musick by Mr. Handel.}}]]|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed for}} I. Walsh{{nb...|in Catharine Street in the Strand, of whom may be had all Mr. Handel’s works.}}|month=March 27 (first performance; Gregorian calendar)<!--Julian calendar: 16 March-->|year=1750|year_published=[1751]|page=17|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=P2UdXgbQZMUC&pg=PA17|oclc=31260514|passage=No Engines can a Tyrant find, / to '''ſtorm''' the Truth-ſupported Mind, {{...}}}}
## {{senseid|en|catch up on production}} {{lb|en|by extension|especially|in command economies}} To catch up (on production output) by making frenzied or herculean efforts.
##: {{ux|en|They were '''storming''' near the end of the month to salvage some goodwill.}}
## {{lb|en|Britain|dialectal|agriculture}} To [[protect]] ([[seed#Noun|seed]]-[[hay#Noun|hay]]) from stormy weather by [[put#Verb|putting]] [[sheaf#Noun|sheaves]] of them into [[small#Adjective|small]] [[stack#Noun|stacks]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive}}
## Of the [[weather#Noun|weather]]: to be [[violent#Adjective|violent]], with strong winds and usually lightning and thunder, and/or hail, rain, or snow.
##* {{RQ:Spenser Shepheardes Calender|chapter=December|folio=50|verso=1|passage=My harueſte haſts to ſtirre vp winter ſterne, / And bids him clayme with rigorous rage hys right. / So nowe he '''ſtormes''' with many a ſturdy ſtoure, / So now his bluſtring blaſt eche [[coast|coſte]] doth ſcoure.}}
##* {{RQ:Homer Chapman Iliads|year=1843|book=I|pages=30–31|pageref=30|passage=[H]e, whose bow thus '''storm'd''' / For our offences, may be calm'd.|footer=A figurative use.}}
##* {{RQ:Drayton Poly-Olbion|chapter=The Tenth Song|page=159|passage=From ''Shetland'' ſtradling vvide, his &#91;[[Boreas]]'s&#93; foote on ''Thuly'' ſets: / VVhence '''ſtorming''', all the vaſt ''Deucalidon'' hee threts, / And beares his boyſtrous vvaues into the narrovver mouth / Of the ''Verginian'' Sea: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Herbert Travaile|edition=4th|page=11|passage=[A]fter a ſhort calm vve obſerved the Ocean firſt to ferment and heave, and then to vvrinkle her ſmooth face, and veering into a contrary romp at length to pull and bluſter, yea next day to '''ſtorm''' ſo outrageouſly, that the Sea men themſelves to my apprehenſion, had ſome fear, {{...}}}}
## To be [[expose]]d to harsh (especially [[cold#Adjective|cold]]) weather.
## {{lb|en|figurative}}
### To [[move#Verb|move]] [[noisily]] and [[quickly]] like a [[storm#Noun|storm]] ''(noun {{senseno|en|weather}})'', usually in a [[state#Noun|state]] of [[anger#Noun|anger]] or [[uproar#Noun|uproar]].
###: {{ux|en|She '''stormed''' out of the room.}}
###* {{RQ:Tennyson Poems 1842|volume=II|poem=The Vision of Sin|page=214|passage=Then the music touch'd the gates and died; / Rose again from where it seem'd to fail, / '''Storm'd''' in orbs of song, a growing gale; {{...}}}}
###* {{RQ:Tennyson Maud|stanza=2|page=152|passage='''Storm'd''' at with shot and shell, / Boldly they rode and well; / Into the jaws of Death, / Into the mouth of Hell, / Rode the six hundred.}}
###* {{quote-journal|en|author=P. Ransome-Wallis|title=Modern Motive Power of the German Federal Railway: Part Two|magazine=[[w:Modern Railways|Trains Illustrated]]|location=London|publisher=w:Ian Allan Publishing|month=October|year=1960|page=611|issn=0141-9935|oclc=35845948|passage=A lovely crisp exhaust: a feeling of almost unlimited power combined with complete freedom of running: and, to crown it all, a most melodious and wholly American chime whistle&mdash;these were my immediate impressions as we '''stormed''' rapidly out of Göttingen, intent on winning back some of the lost time.}}
### {{lb|en|by extension|chiefly|military}} To move quickly in the [[course#Noun|course]] of an assault on a fortified position or stronghold, a building, etc.
