###* {{RQ:Foxe Actes and Monuments|edition=6th|volume=II|chapter=The Life and Storie of the True Seruant and Martyr of God William Tindall: Who for His Notable Paines and Trauell may Well bee Called the Apostle of England in This our Latter Age|page=982|column=1|passage=[T]he prieſts of the countrey cluſtering togither, began to grudge and '''ſtorme''' againſt Tindall &#91;{{w|William Tyndale}}&#93;, rauing againſt him in alehouſes and other places.}}
###* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merchant of Venice Q1|act=I|scene=iii|page=35|passage=VVhy looke you hovv you '''ſtorme''', / I vvould be friends vvith you, and haue your loue, / Forget the ſhames that you haue ſtain'd me vvith, {{...}}}}
###* {{RQ:Homer Chapman Iliads|year=1843|book=V|page=145|passage=O Father, '''storm'st''' thou not / To see us take these wrongs from men?}}
###* {{RQ:Swift Directions to Servants|edition=new|chaptername=Rules|page=102|passage=It often happens, that servants sent on messages are apt to stay out somewhat longer than the message requires, {{...}} when you return, the master '''storms''', the lady scolds; stripping, cudgelling, and turning off is the word. But here you ought to be provided with a set of excuses, enough to serve on all occasions: {{...}}}}
###* {{RQ:Richardson Pamela|volume=I|letter=XXXI|chapter=[Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, the 32d, 33d, and 34th Days of My Imprisonment]|page=239|passage=Bleſs me! ſhe curſes and '''ſtorms''' at me like a Trooper, and can hardly keep her Hands off me.}}
###* {{RQ:Austen Pride and Prejudice|volume=III|page=299|passage=I shall offer to pay him to-morrow; he will rant and '''storm''' about his love for you, and there will be an end of the matter.}}
